  <p><strong>How do fabric minimums in kilograms affect my garment order?</strong></p>

  <h2>Translate kilograms into a usable estimate</h2>
  <p>Suppose a mill minimum is 60 kg and the supplier estimates a gross cut requirement of 0.30 kg per unit for your planned size mix. The starting estimate is 200 garments. This is an illustration, not a Fitclo minimum. Ask whether the estimate already includes cutting loss, fabric shrinkage and defects so you do not add the same allowance twice.</p>

  <h2>Check what can actually be pooled</h2>
  <p>A bra and leggings may share a face fabric, but the bra lining, waistband or trim may have independent minimums. Compare usable width, GSM, colour and finishing before treating two fabrics as interchangeable. If only the colour minimum is the constraint, quote the closest available stock shade separately.</p>

  <h2>Agree what happens to unused fabric</h2>
  <p>Ask whether the quote charges for the full dye lot, how unused material would be recorded, and whether it can be held for a repeat order. Request the fabric code, estimated consumption and price basis in writing.</p>

  <p>Industry reference: <a href="https://jgapparel.co.kr/">J&G describes the fabric specification inputs a supplier can ask for, including width, weight and intended use.</a></p>
